From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: comp.admin.policy,news.admin.policy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,comp.org.eff.talk
Subject:  Re: Policies of the U. of Missouri-Rolla, Computer Services
Date: 4 Jun 1994 20:26:35 -0400
Message-ID: <2sr63r$chk@eff.org>

This is a critique of the policies of the U. of Missouri-Rolla's
Computer Services department. It illegally (in my nonlawyer opinion)
bans trivial Netnews articles, annoying articles, and anonymous
articles. It bans even nonharassing display of might-be-offensive text
or pictures. Read literally, it forbids reading of rec.pets.cats. It
seems to illegally give the Director of Computer Services authority to
summarily punish faculty and students. It offers no privacy
protection.

You can access the policies on-line via gopher:
  gopher mercury.cc.umr.edu
  then select "Departments", "Computing Services", and "Policies"
and WWW
  <gopher://mercury.cc.umr.edu:70/11/Departments/Computing%20Services/Policies>

>===========================================================
>Revision date: March 30, 1994
>Contact: megbrady@umr.edu

>NetNews Usage Guidelines
> 
>[...]
>Under no circumstances should trivial or annoying
>messages be posted nor should faked or anonymous userids be used.

I believe it is illegal for a state university to forbid trivial or
annoying messages in a university forum. I believe it is illegal for a
state university to create a rule that is as vague and overlybroad as
this one. [Disclaimer: I'm not lawyer, but I am enclosing references.]

[...]
>DO NOT display or print pictures that others might find obscene/offensive. 
>DO NOT display or forward text that others might find obscene/offensive.
[...]

While a state university can (and must) forbid harassment, I don't
believe that it can legally forbid display (especially private
display) of what others *might* find offensive. Also, I believe this
rule is unconstitutional vague.

There is more and more fine art available on the Net. This rule would
make a professor or student who looked a Manet's "Olympia" or Dali's
"Young Virgin Autosodomised by her own Chastity" subject to
punishment.

See the referenced faq/computer-porn for information on banning
harassment without banning constitutionally-protected speech.

>DO NOT submit annoying or trivial messages to any news group or user.
>DO NOT submit harmful or harassing messages to any news group or user.

What is a annoying message? What is a trivial message? What is a
harmful Netnews article? What is a harassing Netnews article? I
believe these rules to be too vague.

>DO NOT submit messages using a fake or anonymous userid or domain.
> Include your name/userid/domain in all submissions, on- or off-campus.

The Supreme Court has said:
   "Anonymously pamphlets, leaflets, brochures and even books
    have played an important role in the progress of mankind.
    Persecuted groups and sects from time to time throughout history
    have been able to criticize oppressive practices and laws either
    anonymously or not at all."
     _Tally v. California_, 362 U.S. 60, 64 (1960)

People post to alt.sexual.abuse.recovery pseudonymously for a good
reason. The university should not outlaw this. Instead, it limit
itself to outlawing fraud.

[...]
>=====================================================================
>UMR Computing & Network Facilities Acceptable Use Guidelines

>1994-95
>UMR Computing Services
>114 Math-Computer Science Bldg.
>Rolla, MO 65401
>314.341.4841
>helpdesk@umr.edu

>Things you should know...

>UMR computing and network facilities are provided in order to promote
>and support academic pursuits.  University computing  facilities are
>therefore to be used only for university business, instruction or
>research activities.

This is not true. If it was, they would not subscribe to newsgroup
rec.pets.cats.

[...] 
> - All computer software is protected by the federal copyright law.

This is not true. Public domain software is not protected by federal
copyright law.

[...]
> - Under no circumstances should copyrighted software be distributed
>outside the university through any mechanism, electronic or
>otherwise.

This ignores shareware and GNU software which are copyrighted but also
copyable.

[...]
> - Accessing the private computer files of others, even if those files
>are unprotected, is illegal.

The term "private" is ambiguous. This rule could be read to say that
you looking at the files in my home directory with my permission is
illegal.

> - Do not endanger or interfere with the operation of any computer
>system.

Every time a professor or student sits down at a terminal he or she is
interfering with another students access to that terminal. I suggest
replacing "interfere" with "disrupt".

[...]
>Examples of activities you should avoid:

>        - Changing or altering hardware or software configurations.

Even on your own software?

>        - Game playing or other trivial applications.

What is "trivial"? Is this enforced fairly?

>        - Sending annoying or obscene messages to any other user.

What is "annoying"? What is "obscene"? What if I don't mind getting a
an obscene message from you.

>        - Using computing systems for personal activities that are not
>required for class, research or university business.

This forbids users from reading rec.pets.cats. It also goes against
the spirit of a university:

    Academic institutions exist for the transmission of knowledge, the
    pursuit of truth, the development of students, and the general
    well-being of society. Free inquiry and free expression are
    indispensable to the attainment of these goals. As members of the
    academic community, students should be encouraged to develop the
    capacity for critical judgment and to engage in a sustained and
    independent search for truth.
      -- from Joint Statement on Rights and Freedoms of Students (see refs)

If this rule was applied to the library, students would not be able to
read books for fun.

It is find to give serious activities higher priority, but to ban all
nonserious activities and then to generally allow them, makes
selective enforcement too likely.

>        - Importing software of unknown function and quality.  This is the
>primary mechanism for spreading viruses.

This is vague.

>Disciplinary Action

>Misuse of university computing facilities will be reported to the
>Director of Computing Services.  The Director may impose restrictions
>on the offender's computer access, and report the incident to the Vice
>Chancellor of Student Affairs, the Vice Chancellor of Administrative
>Services, or the Vice Chancellor of Academic Affairs, whichever is
>appropriate.  

This suggests that the Director has claims authority to impose a
summary punishment on professors and students, ignoring regular
university due process procedures. I would be very surprised if the
Director actually has (or can have) this authority legally.  Due
process before punishment is not optional at a state university, it is
the law (and often part of the Student and Faculty codes).

[...]
>================================================================
>[For context, here is some of the Faculty Code at the UMR. - Carl]

>     .0303     Faculty Rights and Responsibilities

>               .01  Academic Rights -- Each faculty member has the right to
>                    freedom of inquiry, discourse, teaching, research and
>                    publication, as well as the responsibilities correlative
>                    with this right (as prescribed by the Board's Rules and
>                    Regulations).

>               .02  Civil Rights -- Faculty members are not required to
>                    relinquish any of their constitutional rights (as
>                    prescribed by the Board's Rules and Regulations).
[...]

I believe the computer policies violate these faculty rights.

- Carl

